---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import rawModels from '../../data/models.json';
import rawPropagators from '../../data/propagators.json';
import landau from '../../../public/examples/weak-landau-damping.metadata.json';
import maxwellWave from '../../../public/examples/maxwell-wave.metadata.json';

const modelCount = (rawModels as any[]).length;
const propagators = rawPropagators as any[];
const couplingCount = propagators.filter((propagator) => propagator.category === 'Coupling').length;
const particleCount = propagators.filter((propagator) => propagator.category === 'Particle').length;
const fieldCount = propagators.filter((propagator) => propagator.category === 'Field').length;
const kineticModels = (rawModels as any[]).filter((model) => model.type === 'Kinetic' || model.type === 'Hybrid').length;
---

<Layout
  title="Numerical methods — Struphy"
  description="An overview of the numerical methods in Struphy: finite element exterior calculus for the fields, particle-in-cell sampling of kinetic species, field–particle coupling, operator splitting in time, and diagnostics for checking the results."
>
  <main>
    <section class="intro" aria-labelledby="page-title">
      <p class="eyebrow">How it works</p>
      <h1 id="page-title">Numerical methods</h1>
      <p class="standfirst">
        A magnetized plasma is described by electromagnetic and fluid fields on physical space and, for kinetic
        species, by a distribution function on position–velocity phase space. Struphy discretizes the fields with
        finite elements and the distribution function with a Monte Carlo (particle-in-cell) representation. This
        page summarizes the two discretizations, their coupling, the time stepping, and the diagnostics available
        for checking a result.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="problem-title">
      <h2 id="problem-title">Two discretizations</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The fields depend on three spatial coordinates and can be represented on a grid. The distribution function{' '}
          <strong>f(x, v, t)</strong> depends on six phase-space coordinates: a grid with 64 points per dimension
          would have 64⁶ ≈ 7·10¹⁰ cells for each species. Struphy therefore represents the fields by finite element
          coefficients and the distribution function by a finite set of weighted markers.
        </p>
        <p>
          A kinetic time step then consists of three kinds of operation: updating the fields, pushing the markers,
          and transferring quantities between the two representations. Whether the coupled system conserves
          energy and charge depends largely on how this transfer is done.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="diagram">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 760 240" role="img" aria-labelledby="pic-title pic-desc">
          <title id="pic-title">Fields on a grid, distribution sampled by markers</title>
          <desc id="pic-desc">
            On the left, electromagnetic fields are represented by spline coefficients on a three-dimensional
            grid. On the right, the six-dimensional distribution function is represented by a cloud of weighted
            markers. Two arrows connect them: fields are evaluated at the marker positions to push the markers,
            and the markers are summed against the same spline basis to build the currents and densities that
            drive the fields.
          </desc>

          <g transform="translate(30 40)">
            <text class="panel-label" x="0" y="-14">Fields — 3D grid</text>
            <rect class="panel" width="230" height="150" />
            {[0, 1, 2, 3].map((i) => <line class="grid" x1={i * 76.6} y1="0" x2={i * 76.6} y2="150" />)}
            {[0, 1, 2].map((i) => <line class="grid" x1="0" y1={i * 75} x2="230" y2={i * 75} />)}
            {[[38, 37], [115, 37], [192, 37], [38, 112], [115, 112], [192, 112]].map(([x, y]) => (
              <circle class="dof" cx={x} cy={y} r="4" />
            ))}
            <text class="panel-note" x="115" y="175" text-anchor="middle">E, B as spline coefficients</text>
          </g>

          <g transform="translate(500 40)">
            <text class="panel-label" x="0" y="-14">Distribution — 6D, sampled</text>
            <rect class="panel" width="230" height="150" />
            {[[42, 30, 5], [88, 62, 3.2], [150, 38, 4.1], [196, 78, 2.4], [60, 104, 3.8], [116, 122, 5.4],
              [176, 116, 3], [28, 72, 2.6], [132, 88, 4.6], [200, 30, 2.2], [72, 20, 2.8], [160, 66, 3.4]].map(
              ([x, y, r]) => <circle class="marker" cx={x} cy={y} r={r} />,
            )}
            <text class="panel-note" x="115" y="175" text-anchor="middle">markers, each carrying a weight</text>
          </g>

          <g transform="translate(0 0)">
            <path class="flow" d="M275 90 C 330 90, 440 90, 495 90" marker-end="url(#tip)" />
            <text class="flow-label" x="385" y="80" text-anchor="middle">evaluate fields at markers</text>
            <path class="flow" d="M495 150 C 440 150, 330 150, 275 150" marker-end="url(#tip)" />
            <text class="flow-label" x="385" y="170" text-anchor="middle">sum markers back onto the basis</text>
          </g>

          <defs>
            <marker id="tip" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse">
              <path d="M0 0L10 5L0 10z" class="tip" />
            </marker>
          </defs>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          Each substep of a kinetic model is a field update, a marker push, or a transfer between the two. The
          {` ${propagators.length} `}propagators are classified accordingly as field, particle and coupling steps
          ({`${fieldCount} / ${particleCount} / ${couplingCount}`}).
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="fields-title">
      <h2 id="fields-title">Fields</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The field equations rely on the identities ∇·(∇×<strong>A</strong>) = 0 and ∇×∇φ = 0. If a
          discretization satisfies them only up to truncation error, a magnetic field obtained as a discrete curl
          has a small non-zero divergence (a spurious magnetic charge), and a discrete gradient field has a small
          non-zero curl (a spurious electric field). Both can act on the plasma as non-physical forces.
        </p>
        <p>
          Such errors accumulate over a run and can show up as artificial heating, spurious drifts or
          instabilities without a physical energy source. Common remedies, such as divergence cleaning, filtering
          or periodic projection, reduce the error after it has been introduced.
        </p>
        <p>
          Struphy instead uses finite element exterior calculus (FEEC). Each field is assigned to one of four
          B-spline spaces forming a discrete de Rham complex: potentials, fields with a curl, fields with a
          divergence, and densities. The spaces are connected by discrete gradient, curl and divergence operators
          whose successive compositions vanish up to round-off. A magnetic field that is initially divergence-free
          therefore remains so, since its time evolution is given by a discrete curl.
        </p>
        <p class="aside">
          <a href="/feec/">FEEC basics</a> shows the four spaces and which model variable sits in each.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="markers-title">
      <h2 id="markers-title">Markers</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A marker is a sample point in phase space that carries a weight; it does not represent a single physical
          particle. The distribution function is approximated by a sum over N weighted markers, and its moments
          (density, current) are obtained as Monte Carlo estimates. A simulation with 10⁵ markers thus samples a
          plasma with a much larger number of physical particles.
        </p>
        <p>
          The number of markers determines the statistical error, which decreases as 1/√N for pseudo-random
          sampling. Markers can alternatively be drawn from low-discrepancy (Sobol) sequences, which can converge
          faster, or placed on a regular tesselation. The initial distribution is specified in physical terms, as a
          Maxwellian with given density and temperature, optionally with a drift or with different parallel and
          perpendicular temperatures.
        </p>
        <p>
          For small perturbations of a known equilibrium f₀, the δf method reduces the noise: only the deviation
          f − f₀ is evolved, and f₀ serves as a control variate. The sampling noise then scales with the amplitude
          of the perturbation rather than with the full distribution, so that linear growth or damping rates can be
          measured with moderate marker numbers.
        </p>
        <p class="aside">
          <a href="/backgrounds/">Kinetic backgrounds</a> are the f₀ you subtract — the distributions themselves,
          with their velocity-space shapes.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="coupling-title">
      <h2 id="coupling-title">Field–particle coupling</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Markers are pushed by the fields, and the fields are driven by currents and densities computed from the
          markers. If the interpolation of the fields to the marker positions and the deposition of marker
          quantities onto the grid are not adjoint to each other, the coupled system does not conserve energy.
        </p>
        <p>
          Struphy uses the same spline basis for both operations. Pushing a marker evaluates the field splines at
          its position. The current or density that drives the fields is obtained by evaluating the <em>same</em>{' '}
          splines at all marker positions and summing over the markers. This is a Monte Carlo quadrature of the
          phase-space integrals in the weak form of the coupled equations, with the markers as quadrature points.
        </p>
        <p>
          Since sampling noise is largest at short wavelengths, the deposited quantities can optionally be filtered
          before they enter the field solve, either with a local three-point stencil or by retaining selected
          toroidal mode numbers.
        </p>
        <p class="aside">
          The {couplingCount} coupling propagators are the steps that do this, across {kineticModels} kinetic and
          hybrid models — see the <a href="/propagators/">propagator catalogue</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="time-title">
      <h2 id="time-title">Time stepping</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A time step is split into substeps (propagators), each corresponding to a distinct physical process: for
          example the rotation of velocities about the magnetic field, the streaming of markers along their
          velocity, the acceleration by the electric field, or the momentum exchange between an energetic species
          and a bulk fluid. Each substep is solved with a method suited to its structure. The rotation about the
          magnetic field, for instance, changes the direction but not the magnitude of the velocity and therefore
          leaves the kinetic energy unchanged for any step size.
        </p>
        <p>
          Composing the substeps sequentially (Lie–Trotter) is first-order accurate in the step size. A symmetric
          composition (Strang), with half-steps, a full step and the half-steps in reverse order, is second-order
          accurate. For long runs, the energy error of a symmetric composition typically oscillates within a
          bounded range instead of drifting in one direction.
        </p>
        <p class="aside">
          <a href="/time-integration/">Time integration</a> shows the splitting for any model, and the
          Runge–Kutta tableaux behind the explicit substeps.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="check-title">
      <h2 id="check-title">Diagnostics and verification</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Each model records its energies as scalar time series: field energy, kinetic energy, thermal energy and
          their sum. Conservation can be assessed by plotting the total energy, and the individual contributions
          show which of them changes.
        </p>
        <p>
          In the electrostatic models, Gauss's law is imposed at t = 0 by solving a Poisson problem for the initial
          electric field; at later times it follows from the discrete scheme. The Vlasov–Maxwell model can record
          the residual as <code>gauss_error</code>, which should remain at round-off level. A growing{' '}
          <code>gauss_error</code> indicates that the discrete charge continuity equation is not satisfied.
        </p>
        <p>
          Several models are additionally verified against known analytic results. The examples list the measured
          value next to the analytic one: a damping rate of {landau.measuredDampingRate.toFixed(4)} for weak
          Landau damping against an exact value of {landau.exactDampingRate}, and a light-wave phase velocity of
          {` ${maxwellWave.measuredPhaseVelocity.toFixed(4)} `}c against {maxwellWave.exactPhaseVelocity} c, both on
          grids coarse enough to run in seconds.
        </p>
        <p class="aside">
          <a href="/examples/">The examples</a> run these end to end, each with its measured result next to the
          analytic one.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section next" aria-labelledby="next-title">
      <h2 id="next-title">Related pages</h2>
      <div class="next-grid">
        <a href="/feec/"><h3>FEEC basics</h3><p>The four spline spaces, and which variable of which model lives in each.</p></a>
        <a href="/backgrounds/"><h3>Kinetic backgrounds</h3><p>The distributions f₀ that kinetic species start from.</p></a>
        <a href="/time-integration/"><h3>Time integration</h3><p>How a model's propagators are composed over a step.</p></a>
        <a href="/propagators/"><h3>Propagators</h3><p>Every substep, what it solves, and which models use it.</p></a>
        <a href="/simulation/"><h3>Building a simulation</h3><p>How the components are assembled into a simulation.</p></a>
        <a href="/models/"><h3>Models</h3><p>The {modelCount} models available in Struphy.</p></a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Layout>

<style>
  .intro{max-width:820px;padding-block:clamp(56px,8vw,92px)}
  .eyebrow{margin:0 0 7px;color:var(--muted);font:600 .76rem/1.4 var(--mono);letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase}
  h1{margin:0 0 20px;font-size:clamp(2.35rem,5vw,4rem);line-height:1.08;letter-spacing:-.035em}
  .standfirst{color:var(--muted);font-size:1.12rem;line-height:1.6}
  .section{padding-block:44px 62px;border-top:1px solid var(--rule)}
  h2{margin:0 0 20px;font-size:clamp(1.4rem,2.6vw,1.85rem)}
  .prose{max-width:720px}
  .prose p{margin:0 0 16px;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.68}
  .prose p:last-child{margin-bottom:0}
  .prose strong{color:var(--paper);font-weight:650}
  .prose em{font-style:italic}
  .aside{margin-top:20px!important;padding-left:14px;border-left:2px solid var(--cyan);font-size:.9rem}
  .aside a{color:var(--cyan)}
  .diagram{max-width:860px;margin:34px 0 0}
  .diagram svg{display:block;width:100%;height:auto}
  .panel{fill:none;stroke:var(--rule)}
  .grid{stroke:var(--rule);stroke-width:.7;opacity:.75}
  .dof{fill:var(--cyan)}
  .marker{fill:var(--cyan);opacity:.55}
  .panel-label{fill:var(--paper);font:650 12px var(--mono)}
  .panel-note{fill:var(--muted);font:11px var(--sans)}
  .flow{fill:none;stroke:var(--muted);stroke-width:1.2}
  .flow-label{fill:var(--muted);font:11px var(--sans)}
  .tip{fill:var(--muted)}
  figcaption{max-width:720px;margin-top:16px;color:var(--muted);font-size:.86rem;line-height:1.55}
  .next-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(240px,1fr));gap:14px;max-width:900px}
  .next-grid a{display:block;padding:16px 18px;border:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--paper);text-decoration:none}
  .next-grid a:hover{border-color:var(--cyan);background:var(--accent-wash)}
  .next-grid h3{margin:0 0 6px;color:var(--cyan);font-size:.95rem}
  .next-grid p{margin:0;color:var(--muted);font-size:.82rem;line-height:1.5}
</style>
